# nr-office-crypto: the new crate mixdog-office-crypto (bounded compound-file reader, password-protection probes, MS-OFFCRYPTO
# decryption). Nothing else consumes it yet: the document-preview port (a later office task) calls it.

## native/core/Cargo.toml

[workspace] members: add, after "crates/mixdog-fsio" (any place in the list):

    "crates/mixdog-office-crypto",

[workspace.dependencies]: add, next to `mixdog-fsio = { path = "crates/mixdog-fsio" }`:

    mixdog-office-crypto = { path = "crates/mixdog-office-crypto" }

The crate's own Cargo.toml names three registry dependencies by version, not through [workspace.dependencies] (nothing else in
the workspace uses them yet); move them into the table if a later crate shares them:

    aes = "0.8.4"            # already in Cargo.lock (0.8.4)
    md-5 = "0.10.6"          # already in Cargo.lock (0.10.6); the library name is `md5`
    quick-xml = { version = "0.38.4", default-features = false }   # cached, NOT yet in Cargo.lock

The fragment of nr-exthost-pdf-codec adds `aes = "0.8"` and `md-5 = "0.10"` to [workspace.dependencies]. When that
fragment is applied (before or with this one), write the two lines of crates/mixdog-office-crypto/Cargo.toml as
`aes = { workspace = true }` and `md-5 = { workspace = true }`, as mixdog-pdf-codec does; both resolve to the same locked
versions (aes 0.8.4, md-5 0.10.6), so the direct versions also build if it is not.

## native/core/Cargo.lock

A new package `mixdog-office-crypto` with dependencies: aes, base64, md-5, mixdog-fsio, mixdog-workspace-hack, quick-xml,
sha1, sha2 (dev: serde_json, tempfile). Every one is in the lock already except `quick-xml 0.38.4` (cached in
C:/Users/tempe/.cargo/registry/cache; its only dependency is memchr, already locked), so one offline
`cargo update -w --offline` (or `cargo metadata --offline` without --locked, once) adds the two entries; the wave builds
`--locked` afterwards.

## native/core/crates/mixdog-workspace-hack/Cargo.toml

Run the repository's hakari check after the member is added and take its output (features of aes, md-5, quick-xml/memchr
are the ones it asks for). Nothing by hand.

## native/core/fixtures/MANIFEST.json

The set `office-crypto` (native/core/fixtures/office-crypto/golden.json and its MANIFEST.json, both added in this commit) is
indexed by `node benchmarks/rust-core/recorders/verify-manifests.mjs --write-index` over the full fixtures tree (the task
worktree has no fixtures checkout, so the index line is the lead's to regenerate).

## SERVICE_PENDING (compose_flip.rs)

No name to remove: this task registers no service and no protocol method.

## What was not compiled

cargo was not run (the crate is not a workspace member yet). Syntax was checked with `rustfmt --check` only. The crates the
code uses and their cached versions: aes 0.8.4 (cipher 0.4), md-5 0.10.6, sha1 0.10, sha2 0.10, base64 0.22, quick-xml 0.38.4
(NsReader::read_resolved_event, resolve_attribute, quick_xml::escape::unescape, BytesDecl::encoding, BytesPI::target,
BytesRef::decode: all read in the 0.38.4 source), mixdog-fsio (File::open, write, read_to_string with `Unlaned` in tests).

## Later tasks

The preview port calls, in this order: `read_encrypted_ooxml(proof, path)` -> `ooxml_encryption(&info)` ->
`KeyDerivation::start(&descriptor, password, &cancel)` / `password_key(..)` -> `package_intact(..)` -> `decrypt_package(..)`;
and `is_encrypted_ooxml`, `is_encrypted_word_document`, `is_encrypted_binary_presentation` for the verdicts. All of them block:
the caller runs them from a lane job.
